# Claude Code. NOTE the trailing /* — with a bare `.claude/` git never descends
# into the directory, so the negation below could never match (the same trap as
# .obsidian/plugins/ and analysis/codex_bridge/ further down). Excluding the
# CONTENTS instead leaves the directory traversable.
#
# CLAUDE.md is tracked because nothing else was keeping it. `.claude/` contains
# a clone of kepano/obsidian-skills, which is where "managed by its own git
# repo" came from — but CLAUDE.md is untracked in that clone and its remote is
# someone else's repository, so the instruction file governing this vault had
# no history and no backup at all. It is the one file in here that is ours,
# hand-written, and irreplaceable.
#
# Everything else stays out, and the exclusion is what keeps it out: API keys in
# settings.local.json, the spend ledger, and the review histories under
# codex-review/, third-opinion/ and tribunal/.
# The negation that was here did not work and made things worse: `.claude/.git`
# is a repository boundary, so `git add .claude/CLAUDE.md` exits 0 and stages
# nothing, while `git add -A` stages `.claude` itself as an embedded-repo
# gitlink. The instructions are mirrored to analysis/vault-instructions.md by
# analysis/scripts/mirror_claude_md.sh instead.
.claude/

# Obsidian — plugin binaries and personal workspace state
# NOTE the trailing /* — with a bare `.obsidian/plugins/` git never descends into
# the directory, so the negations below can never match. Excluding the CONTENTS
# instead leaves the directory traversable.
.obsidian/plugins/*
.obsidian/workspace.json
.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json

# ...except the Jarvis Command Center source, which is hand-written code, not a
# downloaded binary. Tracking it gives it real version history (it previously
# existed only as _jcc_backup_pre_*.js copies) and makes it visible to
# `/codex:review`, which is git-scoped and could not see it otherwise.
!.obsidian/plugins/jarvis-command-center/
!.obsidian/plugins/jarvis-command-center/main.js
!.obsidian/plugins/jarvis-command-center/styles.css
!.obsidian/plugins/jarvis-command-center/manifest.json
!.obsidian/plugins/jarvis-command-center/tribunal.png
# The hand-made rollback copies stay out — they are snapshots of main.js, and
# git is the thing that replaces them.
.obsidian/plugins/jarvis-command-center/_jcc_backup_*

# Sensitive files
Zotero API Key.md

# macOS
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride

# Claude Code — local settings contain API keys
.claude/settings.local.json

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
.env
.venv/

# Raw experimental data — too large for git
data/raw/
*.fcs
*.fastq
*.fastq.gz
*.bam
*.sam
*.bai

# Generated outputs — reproducible from scripts
data/figures/
deadlines/lab-deadlines.ics

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# R
.Rhistory
.Rdata

# Large sequence files
*.ab1
*.abi

# Codex bridge queue. Logs, the live inbox and the pidfile are transient. The
# REPLIES and the PROMPTS THAT PRODUCED THEM are review records and stay tracked:
# a findings file without its brief cannot be audited, because the brief decides
# what Codex looks for. NOTE the trailing /* - git will not descend into a
# directory excluded as `analysis/codex_bridge/`, so the negations below could never match
# (the same trap as .obsidian/plugins/ above).
analysis/codex_bridge/*
!analysis/codex_bridge/outbox/
analysis/codex_bridge/outbox/*
!analysis/codex_bridge/outbox/*.reply.md
!analysis/codex_bridge/outbox/*.meta.json
!analysis/codex_bridge/processed/
analysis/codex_bridge/processed/*
!analysis/codex_bridge/processed/*.md

# Vault semantic index — 20 MB of regenerable binary that churns on every
# rebuild. Two cents to recreate: embed_vault.py build --yes
data/derived/vault_index/

# Word exports. The markdown beside them is the source; a .docx is a zip, so
# every version git stores is ~40 KB of opaque blob that `git diff` can only
# call "binary files differ" - which is the exact thing the
# markdown-is-canonical rule exists to prevent. Regenerate instead:
# analysis/scripts/md_to_docx.py <the .md>. Marked-up copies coming BACK are
# read with analysis/scripts/ingest_docx_comments.py, which writes the comments
# into a tracked markdown note - so the reviewer's edits keep their history
# while the binary does not.
#
# NOTE this ignores nothing already tracked: git only consults .gitignore for
# UNtracked paths. Two files in fellowships/hertz-2026/drafts/ stay tracked on
# purpose, because neither can be rebuilt from anything in the vault - the
# received prompt document `a received prompt document`, and
# `an export whose markdown was never committed`, whose markdown was never committed. An
# irreplaceable document belongs in git even when it is a blob.
fellowships/**/drafts/*.docx
proposals/**/drafts/*.docx

# ...except an ORIGINAL: a document written in Word first, whose markdown was
# converted OUT of it rather than exported INTO it. Deleting one loses work that
# no script can rebuild, so it is tracked despite being a blob — the same
